2. Loaded at runtime, not redistributed
---------------------------------------

wrapper/index.html fetches these from cdn.jsdelivr.net when the dashboard is
opened, the only external origin the Content-Security-Policy in
web_backend/app.py allows. No copy of them is in this repository, so no licence
obligation attaches to it; they are listed because someone running the dashboard
is entitled to know what it pulls in.

    Chart.js 4.4.0            MIT    https://www.chartjs.org
    CodeMirror 5.65.16        MIT    https://codemirror.net/5/
    Phosphor Icons web 2.1.1  MIT    https://phosphoricons.com
    Tailwind CSS 2.2.19       MIT    https://tailwindcss.com

A deployment that must not reach a CDN has to vendor these and tighten that
policy; nothing else in the stack makes an outbound request to serve a page.


3. Dependency licence review
----------------------------

Reviewed 2026-10-07 over the 110 distributions pinned by uv.lock.

    BSD-family       torch, torchvision, numpy, scipy, scikit-learn, pandas,
                     matplotlib, uvicorn, starlette, httpx, Jinja2, click, ...
    MIT              fastapi, pydantic, PyJWT, PyMySQL, DBUtils, PyYAML, pytest,
                     python-multipart, GPUtil, ...
    Apache-2.0       bcrypt, requests, torchmetrics, medmnist, fire, tzdata, ...
    PSF-2.0          typing_extensions
    HPND             pillow
    dual             cryptography (Apache-2.0 OR BSD-3-Clause), packaging

Four entries are not plain permissive and are the reason this section exists.

mysql-connector-python, GPLv2 with the FOSS License Exception
    Oracle's MariaDB/MySQL driver, imported optionally by
    database/connection.py for its connection pooling. PyMySQL (MIT) is the
    driver the Job Manager uses, and the code degrades to no pooling when the
    connector is absent.

    GPLv2 alone would be incompatible with distributing FASTER under MIT. The
    FOSS License Exception Oracle attaches to it is what makes this fine: it
    permits linking from software released under a listed FOSS licence, and MIT
    is on that list. The obligation stays with the driver and does not reach
    FASTER's own source.

deap, LGPL-3.0
    The genetic-programming engine behind FedGP (utils/gp.py). FASTER imports
    it; it is not modified and not statically linked. LGPL's copyleft attaches to
    DEAP's own files, so it constrains neither FASTER's licence nor an
    application built on FASTER. Modifying DEAP itself would change that.

certifi (MPL-2.0) and tqdm (MPL-2.0 AND MIT)
    File-level copyleft: the obligation attaches to their files and only if
    those files are modified, which does not happen here. They impose nothing on
    MIT-licensed source that merely imports them.

Separately, torch pulls in twelve nvidia-*-cu12 CUDA wheels on Linux. These are
NVIDIA proprietary, redistributable under the CUDA EULA but not open source, and
they are copied into the faster-app and job-manager-api images. Anyone
redistributing those images is redistributing them too, under NVIDIA's terms
rather than MIT. A CPU-only build (see the GPU note in the README quickstart)
avoids them entirely.

No GPL or AGPL obligation reaches FASTER's own source. Nothing in the set
constrains redistributing FASTER under the MIT licence.

